\subsection{Phase F: constructive stochastic practical stability}
\label{sec:stability-widening-phase-f}

The stochastic theorem in Sec.~\ref{sec:finite-live-capture} intentionally does
not infer a Gaussian drift inequality from deterministic bounded-input ISS.
With the explicit Phase-B metric and Phase-E deterministic decrease margin, the
missing drift coefficients can instead be derived from conditional noise
moments and a source-map remainder bound.

On a certified Phase-C normal-Live tube, write one exact accepted/rejected
source step as
\begin{equation}
 \vct z_{k+1}
 =\vct f_k(\vct z_k)
 +\mat G_k\vct w_k
 +\vct r_{w,k}(\vct z_k,\vct w_k),
 \label{eq:widen-stochastic-map}
\end{equation}
where $\vct f_k$ is the zero-noise nonlinear map already covered by Phase E,
$\mat G_k\vct w_k$ is the first-order noise channel, and the remaining
noise--geometry interaction satisfies
\begin{equation}
 \|\vct r_{w,k}\|
 \le c_{zw}\|\vct z_k\|\,\|\vct w_k\|
      +c_{ww}\|\vct w_k\|^2.
 \label{eq:widen-stochastic-remainder}
\end{equation}
Such constants exist on every compact accepted-measurement/geodesic source
family because the rotation, gain injection, and reset maps are smooth on each
source branch; branch changes caused by rejected measurements are included as
members of the bounded switching family.  Let
\begin{equation}
 \mathbb E[\vct w_k\mid\mathcal F_k]=0,
 \qquad
 \mathbb E[\vct w_k\vct w_k^\top\mid\mathcal F_k]=\mat\Sigma_k,
 \qquad
 \mat\Sigma_k\preceq\bar\mat\Sigma.
 \label{eq:widen-gaussian-noise}
\end{equation}
For conditionally Gaussian noise define
\begin{align}
 s_2&:=\operatorname{tr}\bar\mat\Sigma,\\
 s_4&:=s_2^2+2\operatorname{tr}(\bar\mat\Sigma^2),
 \label{eq:widen-gaussian-moments}
\end{align}
so that
$\mathbb E\|w_k\|^2\le s_2$ and
$\mathbb E\|w_k\|^4\le s_4$.

Let
$L_f:=\bar A+\ell$, $\bar G:=\sup_k\|\mat G_k\|$, and let $a_0$ be
Eq.~\eqref{eq:widen-a0}.  Define
\begin{align}
 a_s&:=\frac{3a_0}{4}
 -2p_+^{\rm an}L_f c_{zw}\sqrt{s_2}
 -4p_+^{\rm an}c_{zw}^2s_2,
 \label{eq:widen-stochastic-margin}\\
 b_s&:=2p_+^{\rm an}\bar G^2s_2
 +4p_+^{\rm an}c_{ww}^2s_4
 +\frac{4[p_+^{\rm an}L_fc_{ww}]^2}{a_0}s_2^2.
 \label{eq:widen-stochastic-floor}
\end{align}

\begin{theorem}[Constructive Gaussian mean-square practical drift]
\label{thm:widen-stochastic-drift}
Assume Eqs.~\eqref{eq:widen-stochastic-map}--\eqref{eq:widen-gaussian-noise}
and $a_s>0$.  While the source state remains in the certified Phase-C
normal-Live family,
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 \mathbb E[V_{k+1}\mid\mathcal F_k]
 \le(1-\alpha_s)V_k+b_s,
 \qquad
 \alpha_s:=\frac{a_s}{p_+^{\rm an}}>0.}
 \label{eq:widen-stochastic-drift}
\end{equation}
Consequently, over any interval on which the certified source family remains
active,
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 \mathbb E V_k
 \le(1-\alpha_s)^{k-j}\mathbb E V_j
 +\frac{b_s}{\alpha_s}.}
 \label{eq:widen-stochastic-ms}
\end{equation}
Thus the stochastic drift coefficients previously denoted abstractly by
$(\lambda_s,\sigma_s^2)$ may be taken as
\begin{equation}
 \lambda_s=1-\alpha_s,
 \qquad \sigma_s^2=b_s,
 \label{eq:widen-stochastic-coefficients}
\end{equation}
provided the explicit noise-margin condition $a_s>0$ holds.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Set $\vct y_k=\mat G_k\vct w_k+\vct r_{w,k}$.  Phase E gives
$V(f_k(z_k))-V(z_k)\le-a_0\|z_k\|^2$.  Conditional zero mean removes the
cross term between $f_k$ and $G_kw_k$.  From
Eq.~\eqref{eq:widen-stochastic-remainder}, Cauchy--Schwarz, and the Gaussian
moments,
\begin{align}
 \mathbb E\|r_{w,k}\|
 &\le c_{zw}\|z_k\|\sqrt{s_2}+c_{ww}s_2,\\
 \mathbb E\|r_{w,k}\|^2
 &\le2c_{zw}^2\|z_k\|^2s_2+2c_{ww}^2s_4.
\end{align}
Using
$\mathbb E\|y_k\|^2\le2\bar G^2s_2+2\mathbb E\|r_{w,k}\|^2$ and
$\|f_k(z_k)\|\le L_f\|z_k\|$, the remaining cross term is bounded by
$2p_+^{\rm an}L_f\|z_k\|\mathbb E\|r_{w,k}\|$.  Young's inequality spends
$a_0/4$ of deterministic decrease on its $c_{ww}s_2$ component.  Collecting
the state-quadratic and constant terms yields
$-a_s\|z_k\|^2+b_s$.  Since
$V_k\le p_+^{\rm an}\|z_k\|^2$, Eq.~\eqref{eq:widen-stochastic-drift}
follows, and scalar expectation iteration gives
Eq.~\eqref{eq:widen-stochastic-ms}.
\end{proof}

Because Gaussian noise is unbounded, a local nonlinear theorem should not
silently claim almost-sure invariance of a compact tube.  Let
$\tau_{\mathcal T}$ be the first source step that leaves the certified
Phase-C family and let $V_{\partial}>0$ be a Lyapunov level contained in that
family.  Applying the drift bound to the process stopped before the first exit
and using Markov's inequality gives the finite-horizon engineering bound
\begin{equation}
 \mathbb P\!\left(
 \max_{j\le n\le k}V_n\ge V_{\partial}
 \right)
 \le
 \frac{\mathbb E V_j+(k-j)b_s}{V_{\partial}},
 \label{eq:widen-stochastic-exit}
\end{equation}
with sharper supermartingale bounds available when needed.  This separates the
mean-square normal-Live claim from rare excursions that invoke the source's
existing rejection, relock, and hybrid-recovery logic.

\begin{remark}[What Phase F closes]
\label{rem:widen-stochastic-scope}
Phase F removes the need to posit numerical drift coefficients independently of
the deterministic proof: $\lambda_s$ and $\sigma_s^2$ are explicit functions
of the deterministic margin, the declared covariance bound, and the
noise--geometry remainder constants.  It does not turn a regional MEKF into a
global Gaussian system.  Unbounded-noise excursions are explicitly separated
from the normal-Live mean-square result and are handled by an exit probability
plus the hybrid recovery theorem rather than being hidden inside a deterministic
bounded-input assumption.
\end{remark}
